 ### [Topological Sort for Cell Projection](999-topological-sort-for-cell-projection.md)
-Topological Sort for Cell Projection, commonly known as Meshed Polyhedra Visibility Ordering (MPVO), is an object-space algorithm that computes a correct back-to-front visibility ordering for volume rendering on convex meshes composed of convex polyhedral cells. It solves the problem of determining the rendering order required for correct alpha compositing by constructing a directed adjacency graph based on local face-sharing relationships and performing a topological sort. It sets itself apart from its siblings and nearest alternatives by relying exclusively on the mesh topology and convexity to guarantee correctness in $O(n)$ time, avoiding the expensive geometric ray-shooting queries of [XMPVO](1363-xmpvo.md) or the image-space rasterization of [SXMPVO](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md). Unlike [Convex Grid Topological Visibility Sorting](2370-convex-grid-topological-visibility-sorting.md), which is a specific application of this principle, MPVO is the general algorithmic framework described by Williams and Max.
+Topological Sort for Cell Projection (MPVO) is an object-space algorithm that computes a correct back-to-front visibility ordering of convex polyhedral cells in a convex mesh by constructing a directed adjacency graph based on face-sharing topology and viewpoint orientation. It sets itself apart from its siblings, such as [XMPVO](1363-xmpvo.md) and [SXMPVO](944-exact-visibility-ordering-for-cell-projection-volume-renderi.md), by relying exclusively on local topological relations across shared faces rather than global geometric ray-shooting queries or image-space rasterization, provided the mesh is convex and acyclic.
 
