 ## Background
-Cell projection was developed at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory by Peter Williams, Claudio Silva, Richard Cook, and Peter Max [S1, S2]. It was introduced as one of the four main paradigms of volume rendering (alongside raycasting, splatting, and shear-warp) [S3, unofficial]. The method was designed to handle curvilinear and unstructured grids directly, avoiding the need for resampling [S1][S4, book]. Key developments include the handling of non-planar faces by subdividing hexahedra into tetrahedra [S4, book], the use of texture mapping to avoid Mach bands [S1, S2], and the development of efficient visibility sorting algorithms like MPVO [S1][S3, unofficial]. It has been parallelized for distributed memory systems [S1][S4, book] and optimized for GPU execution on structured data [S1].
+Cell projection was developed at Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory by Max, Williams, Silva, and Cook [S1, S2]. It is one of the four main paradigms of volume rendering, alongside raycasting, splatting, and shear-warp [S3, unofficial]. The method was designed to handle curvilinear and unstructured grids directly, avoiding the resampling required by grid-based methods [S1][S4, book]. It has been parallelized for distributed-memory systems and optimized for GPU execution on structured data [S1][S4, book].
 
